 type(s) for *: 'NoneType' and 'int'\n```\n\nTrack down why this happens and fix it so the program runs cleanly. When it\nworks, `python3 main.py` should print exactly:\n\n```\nTotal: 5.0\n```\n\nDone when `python3 main.py` exits successfully and prints that line.\n"], "completed": true, "dotnet_build_calls": 0, "edit_calls": 1, "ended_on_rejection": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "stdout", "first_after_build": false, "first_side": "without", "fresh_subagents": 1, "harness": "opencode", "harness_version": "0.0.0--202610170355 (/workspace/openbench-ab/results/opencode-src/bin/64e2bf8bf00eca1c6ad4486e29eb84f7907d1d35/opencode)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": true, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": null, "model_max_tokens": null, "output_tail": "[tool: task]\n[tool: task]\n[tool: read]\n[tool: edit]\n[tool: bash]\nI fixed the crash. `python3 main.py` now exits cleanly and prints `Total: 5.0`.\n\nThe cause was a typo in `settings.json:2`. The key was spelled `\"rat\"` instead of `\"rate\"`. `app/pricing.py:8` calls `SETTINGS.get(\"rate\")`, which returns `None` for a missing key. That `None` reached `rate * count` in `app/report.py:9` and raised the `TypeError`.\n\nI traced this with a general subagent. I then resumed the same subagent session by its task id and asked which settings key was missing. It confirmed the key was `\"rate\"`. I renamed `\"rat\"` to `\"rate\"` myself.\n\nI didn't change `get_rate()`. Switching it to `SETTINGS[\"rate\"]` would make a missing key raise a clear `KeyError` at the source instead of a later `TypeError`.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "params_stripped": ["param:temperature"], "params_stripped_requests": 3, "permission_rejec